# Nothing is needed here to start. Open Claudio, sign in with the owner token printed in the
# container's log (`docker compose logs claudio`), then sign in to Claude in the setup assistant.
#
# Headless only (a deployment that cannot open a browser): a long-lived token for your subscription,
# made on a machine where you are logged in with `claude setup-token`, or a pay-per-use API key.
# They take precedence over an account signed in from the app.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Host port for the API + UI
PORT=8797

# Claude Code version baked into the image. Unset, the Dockerfile's pinned version is used; set an
# exact version to move it, or `latest` / `stable` to give up the pin. The System page tells you when
# a newer one exists, and installs it from the app. Rebuild after changing it.
# CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION=

# Daily check for a newer Claude Code (reads the npm registry). `off` for an air-gapped install.
# CLAUDIO_CLI_UPDATE_CHECK=off

# Authentication for the API. Left unset, the container makes an owner token on its first start and
# prints it once in its log. Set these to choose the token yourself, before publishing the port
# beyond this machine, and for the `tls` compose profile (docker compose --profile tls up -d).
# CLAUDIO_AUTH_MODE=none opts out of the first-run token (the terminal and in-app updates then need
# a token you turn on later).
# CLAUDIO_AUTH_MODE=token
# CLAUDIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Lost the token and cannot reach the data volume? Set a new CLAUDIO_AUTH_TOKEN, add this and restart:
# the new value replaces the stored one (the audit log records it with actor `env`). A value already
# applied is not applied again, so leaving it set does not undo a token rotated later.
# CLAUDIO_AUTH_TOKEN_RESET=1
# Public name the `tls` profile serves (a real name gets a Let's Encrypt certificate)
# CLAUDIO_DOMAIN=claudio.example.com

# Host names this wrapper answers to, besides loopback. A request whose `Host` is not one of these
# is refused with 421 before its credential is even looked at, which is what stops a page on someone
# else's domain from rebinding that name to 127.0.0.1 and driving the API from your browser.
# Reaching it on localhost needs nothing here. Reaching it through the `tls` profile does: put the
# same name as CLAUDIO_DOMAIN, because Caddy passes the Host through unchanged and everything would
# otherwise answer 421. Comma-separated, ports and letter case ignored.
# An entry may be a `*.domain` pattern instead of a name, for a deployment whose host is not fixed —
# a tunnel that mints a new one on every start, a preview environment per branch. It stands for the
# subdomains and never for the domain itself, so list that separately if it is served too.
# CLAUDIO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=claudio.example.com,*.tunnel.example

# The tunnel through Tailscale (Settings > Remote access): `tailscale serve` on this container's own
# tailnet name, reachable only from the person's own tailnet, never Funnel. On by default: the image
# starts a tailscaled of its own (userspace networking, state in the data volume) and you sign it in
# from the app, with a login URL or an auth key. Once open it reaches the server on 127.0.0.1 inside
# the container, around the 127.0.0.1-only port above and the `tls` profile's proxy. It refuses to
# open while CLAUDIO_AUTH_MODE is `none`, and only opens when someone starts it from the UI or turns
# on "start with Claudio" there. `off` starts no tailscaled and offers no tunnel (outside Docker too).
# CLAUDIO_TUNNEL=off
# The node's name on the tailnet (`claudio` by default; Tailscale adds a number if it is taken).
# CLAUDIO_TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME=claudio
# The HTTPS port of Claudio's Serve rule on the tailnet name (8444 by default), kept off 443 so a
# Serve rule of your own is never touched.
# CLAUDIO_TUNNEL_PORT=8444

# Browsers allowed to call this API from another origin (comma-separated, or `*` to echo whoever
# asks). The bundled UI never needs it: it is served from this same origin. The event streams obey
# this list too.
# CLAUDIO_CORS_ORIGIN=https://panel.example.com

# Consecutive failed health probes (30 s apart) after which the container restarts itself
# CLAUDIO_HEALTH_RESTART_AFTER=3

# Permission mode for runs that do not specify one.
# The container is the sandbox, so the image defaults to bypassPermissions.
# CLAUDIO_DEFAULT_PERMISSION_MODE=bypassPermissions

# Max simultaneous `claude` processes
# CLAUDIO_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=8
